# =============================================================================
# pynq_z2_ts.xdc -- constraints for ts_top on PYNQ-Z2
#
# Device : xc7z020clg400-1     Board part : tul.com.tw:pynq-z2:part0:1.0
# Vivado : 2024.1  (must match PYNQ 3.1 -- the board runs kernel xilinx-v2024.1)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE IS NO create_clock HERE
#
# The whole design runs on ONE clock: PS FCLK_CLK0, configured to 200 MHz in the
# block design (build_ts.tcl). That clock is generated by the PS7 PLL and its
# constraint comes from the PS7 IP itself -- it is NOT an external pin, so there
# is nothing for this file to constrain.
#
# That single clock domain is the point of the design (see README_PL.md sec.2):
# timestamp counter, tau registers and the AXI4-Lite slave all share one time
# base, so there is no CDC anywhere.
#
# The board's 125 MHz oscillator (H16) is deliberately NOT constrained: the
# design does not use it. Adding a pin constraint for an unused clock is a
# common way to get a phantom timing failure.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ASCII ONLY. This file is kept free of non-ASCII characters on purpose --
# a toolchain parsing a UTF-8 file on a zh-CN Windows codepage is exactly the
# kind of thing that fails silently and is miserable to debug.
# =============================================================================

# --- configuration bank voltage -------------------------------------------
# PYNQ-Z2 has MIO bank 0 / PL bank 34 at 3.3 V. Without these two properties
# Vivado warns and the generated configuration can be wrong.
set_property CFGBVS VCCO       [current_design]
set_property CONFIG_VOLTAGE 3.3 [current_design]

# --- debug LEDs ------------------------------------------------------------
# PYNQ-Z2 on-board LEDs LD0..LD3. Pins verified against Xilinx's own
# xup_fpga_vivado_flow lab1_pynq.xdc (source/pynq-z2/lab1/lab1_pynq.xdc).
#
# These LEDs are ACTIVE HIGH on PYNQ-Z2: driving 1 lights them. ts_top drives
# them directly (no inversion) -- see the dbg_led block at the end of ts_top.v.
#
#   LD0 = run (counter running)      LD1 = FIFO empty (PS keeping up)
#   LD2 = FIFO full (PS too slow)    LD3 = samples were lost (sticky, W1C 0x50)
#
# The port name is 'dbg_led'. In the block design the external port created for
# ts_top's dbg_led output must also be named 'dbg_led' so that this constraint
# matches; build_ts.tcl does exactly that.

set_property -dict {PACKAGE_PIN R14 IOSTANDARD LVCMOS33} [get_ports {dbg_led[0]}]
set_property -dict {PACKAGE_PIN P14 IOSTANDARD LVCMOS33} [get_ports {dbg_led[1]}]
set_property -dict {PACKAGE_PIN N16 IOSTANDARD LVCMOS33} [get_ports {dbg_led[2]}]
set_property -dict {PACKAGE_PIN M14 IOSTANDARD LVCMOS33} [get_ports {dbg_led[3]}]

# --- external sample-strobe pins -------------------------------------------
# ts_top has a 4-bit input 's_valid' (external sample-arrival strobes).
#
# At present nothing drives it: the SRC register is written to 0xF so all four
# modalities use ts_top's INTERNAL strobe generator (rtl/strobe_gen.v), which
# needs no pins at all. So 's_valid' is intentionally left unconstrained and
# tied off in the block design.
#
# When real sensors are connected, do NOT simply constrain s_valid to four
# random pins -- the four strobes arrive from different physical devices and
# each needs its own input delay (set_input_delay) against the 200 MHz period,
# otherwise the timestamp of an edge is not trustworthy to a cycle. That is a
# separate piece of work.
